#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175795 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175946 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dbed47b-77e4-53fd-8eb9-17ec13e42a3e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of a Given Rank"}] :question/id #uuid "13cf1317-c3a1-5175-9f5b-c3da13cad348" :question/math-academy-id "e-6744" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda) = (\\lambda-4)^6(\\lambda-9)^8,$ the rank of $(A-9I)^3$ equals $9,$ and the rank of $(A-9I)^4$ equals $7.$ If a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^{14}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 9?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda = 9,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ is a vector $\\mathbf{v}$ such that \n\n$$\n(A - 9I)^4 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}\n\\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad\n(A - 9I)^3 \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ must be a $14 \\times 14$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-9I)^3$ equals $9,$ then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-9I)^3 = 14-9 = 5.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A - 9I)^3 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^3$ of dimension $5$ in $\\mathbb{R}^{14}.$\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-9I)^4$ equals $7,$  then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-9I)^4 = 14-7 = 7.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A - 9I)^4 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^4$ of dimension $7$ in $\\mathbb{R}^{14}.$\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^3$ is a subspace of dimension $5$ in $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^4$, which is a subspace of dimension $7,$ our basis can contain only $2$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^4$ but not in $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^3.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-9I)^4 - \\text{nullity}(A-9I)^3 = 7-5=2\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175835 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175984 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfc9161a-5f14-5c16-afb0-832f81f30be4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of Rank Greater Than One"}] :question/id #uuid "faa37d23-bdd2-5651-bf53-47835ee61cc8" :question/math-academy-id "e-9055" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda) = (1+\\lambda) (4-\\lambda)^3(\\lambda-1)^5,$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 1,$ there are exactly $3$ eigenvectors. If a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^{9}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 1?$" :question/worked-solution "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda$ equals the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda.$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda) = (1+\\lambda) (4-\\lambda)^3(\\lambda-1)^5,\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda = 1$ has multiplicity $5.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 1$ is $5.$\n\nHowever, there are only $3$ eigenvectors corresponding to  the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 1,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 1$ is $3.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda = 1$ is $5-3=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175844 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175993 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85ecbb00-cabe-5838-9ac4-50ebc8821c05" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of a Given Rank Using Nullity"}] :question/id #uuid "bed5f45f-5c76-542b-965f-eadf474823c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-9125" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda) = (3+\\lambda)^3(\\lambda-\\pi)^8(\\lambda-2)^3,$ the nullity of $(A-\\pi I)^3$ equals $4,$ and the nullity of $(A-\\pi I)^4$ equals $6.$ If a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^{14}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = \\pi?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda = \\pi,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ is a vector $\\mathbf{v}$ such that \n\n$$\n(A - \\pi I)^4 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}\n\\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad\n(A - \\pi I)^3 \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A-\\pi I)^3$ has dimension $4.$ This means that the solutions to $(A - \\pi I)^3 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ form a subspace of dimension $4.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A-\\pi I)^4$ has dimension $6.$ This means that the solutions to $(A - \\pi I)^4 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ form a subspace of dimension $6.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(A-\\pi I)^3$ is a subspace of dimension $4$ in $\\text{Null}(A-\\pi I)^4$, which is a subspace of dimension $6,$ our basis can contain only $2$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(A-\\pi I)^4$ but not in $\\text{Null}(A-\\pi I)^3.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-\\pi I)^4 - \\text{nullity}(A-\\pi I)^3 = 6-4=2\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=\\pi.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386445 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175984 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfc9161a-5f14-5c16-afb0-832f81f30be4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of Rank Greater Than One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "195c4aec-4206-524d-999f-7c4101d2d7fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386447} {:answer/id #uuid "ba293136-d659-561c-8537-09a5722e5b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386448} {:answer/id #uuid "96a612d3-8f1e-5786-ae51-b97758955c46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386449} {:answer/id #uuid "52d296bc-bcdf-549e-baff-a4abe5bc23b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386450} {:answer/id #uuid "61959f7f-7993-5031-83b4-69a5c25709c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96a612d3-8f1e-5786-ae51-b97758955c46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386449} :answer-field/id #uuid "480350c9-2945-54c3-8987-b479d87ce01c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386446}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fdcb3137-9f9a-53d4-9695-935580d7ee98" :question/math-academy-id "q-109666" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(\\lambda +1)^{3}(\\lambda -3)^{2}(\\lambda +2)^{4},$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1,$ there are exactly $2$ eigenvectors. If a basis of ${R}^{9}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1?$" :question/worked-solution "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is equal to the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda .$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda )=(\\lambda +1)^{3}(\\lambda -3)^{2}(\\lambda +2)^{4},\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda =-1$ has multiplicity $3.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is $3.$\n\nHowever, there are only $2$ eigenvectors corresponding to  the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is $2.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-1$ is $3-2=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362689 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175984 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfc9161a-5f14-5c16-afb0-832f81f30be4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of Rank Greater Than One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e23481a5-a245-58f5-9277-aa451e3cd5e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362691} {:answer/id #uuid "d1e228ad-32ee-5207-8f11-ace4458ad975" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362692} {:answer/id #uuid "ef5bedc9-906b-5fdf-afbc-28280706a045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362693} {:answer/id #uuid "d092f86e-9313-538f-8643-4caea5dfbfa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362694} {:answer/id #uuid "58e83042-c2b9-530f-b764-75ead7c985da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e23481a5-a245-58f5-9277-aa451e3cd5e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362691} :answer-field/id #uui