/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173741} {:answer/id #uuid "fa1ab9c9-85f4-531d-b3a7-bea26e2d3c0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173742} {:answer/id #uuid "3c42c8d8-a86c-5a0e-9310-317060bb58bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173743} {:answer/id #uuid "5029f1b5-3ff3-57ee-bad8-a1511f8c44c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173744} {:answer/id #uuid "69acfa87-eb10-5b78-aa51-34879b469042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa1ab9c9-85f4-531d-b3a7-bea26e2d3c0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173742} :answer-field/id #uuid "575ab359-7284-535c-aa54-794bcba6804d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3cb467c0-ea77-596f-b372-4352d634576f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109740" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $D$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{3}(4-\\lambda )^{2}(7-\\lambda )^{6},$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0,$ there are exactly $2$ eigenvectors. If a basis of ${R}^{11}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $D,$ how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0?$" :question/worked-solution "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is equal to the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda .$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{3}(4-\\lambda )^{2}(7-\\lambda )^{6},\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda =0$ has multiplicity $3.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ is $3.$\n\nHowever, there are only $2$ eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ is $2.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda =0$ is $3-2=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362703 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175993 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85ecbb00-cabe-5838-9ac4-50ebc8821c05" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of a Given Rank Using Nullity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89f12690-3a63-551c-b510-20f96310bef2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362705} {:answer/id #uuid "64df770c-14e6-51b7-b5e2-6609c8157928" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362706} {:answer/id #uuid "085b8a3b-48a3-5558-81c6-03cca47694ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362707} {:answer/id #uuid "ffb5aedb-535f-5de6-97b4-1fe57d43401d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362708} {:answer/id #uuid "929cd437-ce88-5c2a-a8a9-5a59a7eab4c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "929cd437-ce88-5c2a-a8a9-5a59a7eab4c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362709} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0070d7b-e6f1-5c2a-b236-0cd78dc7b53b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4952e6c8-6c33-5b76-932a-88fabacd27b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-109753" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $C$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(4+\\lambda )(\\lambda -3)^{4}(\\lambda +6)^{17},$ the nullity of $(C+6I)^{3}$ equals $2,$ and the nullity of $(C+6I)^{4}$ equals $6.$ If a basis of ${R}^{22}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $C$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-6?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $C$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =-6,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(C+6I)^{4}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(C+6I)^{3}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(C+6I)^{3}$ has dimension $2.$ This means that the solutions to $(C+6I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $2.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(C+6I)^{4}$ has dimension $6.$ This means that the solutions to $(C+6I)^{4}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $6.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(C+6I)^{3}$ is a subspace of dimension $2$ in $\\text{Null}(C+6I)^{4}$, which is a subspace of dimension $6,$ our basis can contain only $4$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(C+6I)^{4}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(C+6I)^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(C+6I)^{4}-\\text{nullity}(C+6I)^{3}=6-2=4\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176251 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175993 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85ecbb00-cabe-5838-9ac4-50ebc8821c05" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of a Given Rank Using Nullity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f99e9009-e6c2-582d-90ae-d0bfcf1f5d58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362725} {:answer/id #uuid "99d135b5-1334-5d73-b629-c04ae8d41c33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362726} {:answer/id #uuid "2dac6776-db44-5e53-8d0e-7467411d2060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362727} {:answer/id #uuid "ac89cd1b-b62b-50dd-b0a5-0452ce5ed0f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362728} {:answer/id #uuid "34ffef47-4e58-50ef-b079-896389f7f959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f99e9009-e6c2-582d-90ae-d0bfcf1f5d58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362725} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd562744-db8f-593a-9c8b-77f2c75d981e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27ab5139-4b6b-5639-b90d-136b8a14f7bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-109843" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{2}(\\lambda +1)^{16}(\\lambda -8)^{2},$ the nullity of $(A+I)^{4}$ equals $4,$ and the nullity of $(A+I)^{5}$ equals $7.$ If a basis of ${R}^{20}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $5$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $5$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(A+I)^{5}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A+I)^{4}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A+I)^{4}$ has dimension $4.$ This means that the solutions to $(A+I)^{4}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $4.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A+I)^{5}$ has dimension $7.$ This means that the solutions to $(A+I)^{5}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $7.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(A+I)^{4}$ is a subspace of dimension $4$ in $\\text{Null}(A+I)^{5}$, which is a subspace of dimension $7,$ our basis can contain only $3$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(A+I)^{4}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(A+I)^{5}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A+I)^{5}-\\text{nullity}(A+I)^{4}=7-4=3\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $5$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176252 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175946 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dbed47b-77e4-53fd-8eb9-17ec13e42a3e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of a Given Rank"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "519aff8a-0f6a-5132-8a28-63205c2a9b41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362799} {:answer/id #uuid "7e2c9519-f70f-51b9-82ab-52d0dbaf22ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362800} {:answer/id #uuid "c7048870-5f85-522e-bdaa-af8db4e61edc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362801} {:answer/id #uuid "1b1c84a6-d5a8-554b-9862-b205e66c5f3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362802} {:answer/id #uuid "b4930134-8b4b-5956-805b-10a2435ebb5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7048870-5f85-522e-bdaa-af8db4e61edc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362801} :answer-field/id #uuid "78419c12-db9b-50fa-ad3a-2e955bd295b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "11749f57-f853-5eba-906e-45841d04e575" :question/math-academy-id "q-109922" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $B$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(\\lambda +\\sqrt{2})^{3}(\\lambda -2)^{21},$ the rank of $(B-2I)^{6}$ equals $4,$ and the rank of $(B-2I)^{5}$ equals $7.$ If a basis of ${R}^{24}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $B$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $6$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $B$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $6$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(B-2I)^{6}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(B-2I)^{5}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $B$ must be a $24\\times 24$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(B-2I)^{5}$ equals $7,$ then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B-2I)^{5}=24-7=17.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(B-2I)^{5}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(B-2I)^{5}$ of dimension $17$ in ${R}^{24}.$\n\n- Since the rank of $(B-2I)^{6}$ equals $4,$  then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B-2I)^{6}=24-4=20.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(B-2I)^{6}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(B-2I)^{6}$ of dimension $20$ in ${R}^{24}.$\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(B-2I)^{5}$ is a subspace of dimension $17$ in $\\text{Null}(B-2I)^{6}$, which is a subspace of dimension $20,$ our basis can contain only $3$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(B-2I)^{6}$ but do not lie in $\\text{Null}(B-2I)^{5}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B-2I)^{6}-\\text{nullity}(B-2I)^{5}=20-17=3\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $6$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559872 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175946 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dbed47b-77e4-53fd-8eb9-17ec13e42a3e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of a Given Rank"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "169df782-f22f-53ee-888a-ef5dd9042bbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559066} {:answer/id #uuid "2155da94-081e-506a-911b-03855a5f2cbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559096} {:answer/id #uuid "d0e04b3f-f522-5411-be37-966761ea2f01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559524} {:answer/id #uuid "dbcd5536-0adc-5f39-92ab-7c7a8048c753" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559557} {:answer/id #uuid "e8356a21-b88d-5892-a5fb-1e440a90d6f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "169df782-f22f-53ee-888a-ef5dd9042bbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559066} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4be26dd-2c06-59f0-a37a-9169047bccde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e1e17bba-2392-58cc-bb67-1a8adce57d23" :question/math-academy-id "q-109927" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $D$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(\\lambda -7)^{3}(\\lambda +4)^{2}(\\lambda -9)^{9},$ the rank of $(D-9I)^{2}$ equals $11,$ and the rank of $(D-9I)^{3}$ equals $8.$ If a basis of ${R}^{14}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $D$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =9?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $D$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =9,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(D-9I)^{3}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(D-9I)^{2}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $D$ must be a $14\\times 14$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(D-9I)^{2}$ equals $11,$ then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(D-9I)^{2}=14-11=3.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(D-9I)^{2}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(D-9I)^{2}$ of dimension $3$ in ${R}^{14}.$\n\n- Since the rank of $(D-9I)^{3}$ equals $8,$  then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(D-9I)^{3}=14-8=6.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(D-9I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(D-9I)^{3}$ of dimension $6$ in ${R}^{14}.$\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(D-9I)^{2}$ is a subspace of dimension $3$ in $\\text{Null}(D-9I)^{3}$, which is a subspace of dimension $6,$ our basis can contain only $3$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(D-9I)^{3}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(D-9I)^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(D-9I)^{3}-\\text{nullity}(D-9I)^{2}=6-3=3\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176567 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175984 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfc9161a-5f14-5c16-afb0-832f81f30be4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of Rank Greater Than One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84e7677b-e65b-5ba2-88a8-2bd2acc82160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175541} {:answer/id #uuid "d6bbab60-450c-5620-a2e5-32645332926c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175542} {:answer/id #uuid "0990d12f-cfc1-56df-8402-1297de8e7a0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175543} {:answer/id #uuid "10c7f86f-3c81-5a11-bcd4-52e71a004a22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175544} {:answer/id #uuid "577a436a-e117-5f95-9bc1-fba890d25d24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10c7f86f-3c81-5a11-bcd4-52e71a004a22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175544} :answer-field/id #uuid "daa3a7e0-2f97-5889-a2d5-5385c643040a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175540}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "07ef05dc-0a2c-5317-9865-4e3852ecab01" :question/math-academy-id "q-78129" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{2}(3-\\lambda )^{4}(5+\\lambda )^{6},$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5,$ there are exactly $2$ eigenvectors. If a basis of ${R}^{12}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5?$" :question/worked-solution "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is equal to the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda .$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{2}(3-\\lambda )^{4}(5+\\lambda )^{6},\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda =-5$ has multiplicity $6.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ is $6.$\n\nHowever, there are only $2$ eigenvectors corresponding to  the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ is $2.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-5$ is $6-2=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364420 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175984 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfc9161a-5f14-5c16-afb0-832f81f30be4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of Rank Greater Than One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e865e05-d063-5259-9427-8e9ac3415420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364422} {:answer/id #uuid "e4d54eb7-8c94-5ba1-8ff1-0e33e8e8ff4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364423} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca18b90-c676-50ef-bb00-24c4e41c4b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364424} {:answer/id #uuid "61161b93-ea6b-5325-aa7b-d6e1119b0a59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364425} {:answer/id #uuid "1c60cb57-ff4f-5029-8ce2-b8fe29a39c9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e865e05-d063-5259-9427-8e9ac3415420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364422} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7b1e17b-e37b-5c86-92c1-41321a65eb42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "246e62ce-be4d-58bc-8aa3-d8a9e5b8b661" :question/math-academy-id "q-78463" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $B$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(1+\\lambda )^{4}(1-\\lambda )^{2}(4-\\lambda )^{3},$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1,$ there is exactly $1$ eigenvector. If a basis of ${R}^{9}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $B$, how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1?$" :question/worked-solution "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is equal to the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda .$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda )=(1+\\lambda )^{4}(1-\\lambda )^{2}(4-\\lambda )^{3},\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda =-1$ has multiplicity $4.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is $4.$\n\nHowever, there is only $1$ eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is $1.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-1$ is $4-1=3.$"}]}
